  "note": "One entry per check. Each scope carrying the property has a floor under it; scopes whose size is a fact about the corpus rather than about the property are listed under context and carry none. Re-recorded in full at 3.10 from a run on the date above. They had last been raised at 2.6 and had stood through thirteen landed checkpoints, so fixture-replay was floored at 568 against 1,296 and 728 expectations could have been deleted while every floor held. Three scopes were deliberately not raised because their value legitimately falls: carried-obligations' reconciled count falls as due points land, ci-parity runs one interpreter on the runners that are not Windows, and writer-ownership's multi-writer count is an exemption count where a fall is tightening. Extended at 3.14: `stated-counts` gains the two claims that derive BUILD_PLAN's permit figure from the fixture, and `fixture-replay`'s \"checkpoints with expectations in the fixture\" becomes \"checkpoints asked done condition seven\", floored at 38 against the old 27. The rename is the point rather than a tidy: the old scope counted the checkpoints the fixture holds something for, which is the set that excludes every checkpoint under a permit, so the figure beside it read 29 of 29 while eight landed checkpoints carried no verification and were named nowhere in the run. A floor on a scope that cannot see the hole is a floor holding the wrong thing, which is the failure the per-scope rule was written for, one level in. Extended again at 3.14: `carried-obligations` moves from 56 to 71 named due points and from 5 to 7 declared ones, and neither is growth in the record. The pattern read a literal `due ` optionally followed by `at `, so it could not see the forms `due before 5.1`, `Due **4.1**`, `due **at 3.6**`, `Due at **the operator**` or a phrase wrapped across a line break, and 6 of the 71 were invisible. One of the six was a real obligation in no table row, so the floor at 56 sat above a scope that had never held the property and would have kept sitting there. The seventh declared due point is that obligation now written down. `stated-counts` gains the obligations table's own total, which was prose reading fifty-eight over fifty-nine rows. Lowered at 4.1: surface-claims' reverse read was recorded at 28 against a pattern that asked only that a sentence hold a surface word somewhere and a visibility verb somewhere, and half of what it matched was prose that happened to hold both. The pattern now requires the verb within thirty characters of the surface it names, which matches 16, and the twelve it dropped were sentences like \"the one thing that was prose\" and \"belongs to a check\". The old figure did not hold because a scope answered by its own exemption list is a scope measuring the exemption list; the property is unchanged and what shrank is the noise. Produced by running the check on 2026-09-01 with each pattern in turn and reading both lists. Five stated-counts scopes removed at 4.1: the four figures BUILD_PLAN's 4.1 classification stated, plus the obligations total it stated alongside them. 4.1 discharged both its rows, so the pile is nought and its section is now a record of what the two were rather than a classification with counts in it. Nothing is unguarded by the removal: the same four figures are asserted of the 4.6 pile, and the table's own total is still read from the 4.6 opening sentence under \"stated again at 4.6\". Produced by reading which claims the section still supports on 2026-09-01 rather than by writing a sentence to keep the old ones passing, which is what the guard that caught this would have been satisfied by.",
